# Copy to .esuna.conf at your Git repository root.
# Simple key=value lines; whitespace and full-line # comments are allowed.
# Unknown/duplicate keys and invalid values are rejected. No inline comments.
# Reviewer identifiers are literal CLI names, not version-specific model names.
# Order matters for --chain. Defaults to all four; the author is excluded.
reviewer_pool=claude,codex,muse,grok

# Finite confidence in [0,1]. Only P0 findings at or above this threshold block,
# and only with --gate. Plain esuna is advisory (exit 0 after a successful review).
gate_threshold=0.7

# Optional; --author-model wins, including an explicit --author-model="".
# author_model=claude

# Review defaults: independent reviewers with merged findings (--merge).
# --chain runs reviewers sequentially in reviewer_pool order instead.
# --verify uses the first remaining reviewer to re-check findings before scoring.
# Overall confidence = mean finding confidence, or 1.0 for no findings.
# Review base = upstream merge-base, else HEAD^, else HEAD for an initial commit.
# Staged and unstaged changes are included; untracked files are not included.
# A repository must have at least one commit.
# --with-tests asks reviewers to include test code/coverage, otherwise guidance
# focuses on production defects (test files remain available as evidence).

# Hook installation: esuna install-hook [--target <repo-root>] [--gate]
# Requires esuna on PATH. Respects Git worktrees and core.hooksPath.
# Existing hooks are refused: merge manually; no backups or overwrites.
# Default hook is advisory, even on tool errors; --gate propagates esuna's status.
# The hook runs this same review scope; it does not interpret remote push refs.
